UI primitives, deliberately mirroring tandem-mobile's §10 subset
==================================================================

`Input`, `FormError`, `EmptyState`, `LoadingState`, `ErrorState` and `Button` here are the web
equivalents of tandem-mobile's `src/components/{Input,ErrorState,EmptyState,LoadingState,
Button}.tsx`.

`@tandem/shared` ships no React and must not start shipping one: a shared component library
would couple an Expo (React Native) renderer and a DOM renderer through one package, which is
not something either platform's rendering primitives (`<View>`/`<Text>` vs `<div>`/`<p>`) can
honestly share. The survey that opened this task found two parallel, independently-transcribed
copies of the same handful of primitives growing across the two repos - not fixed by sharing
code, so it is fixed the only way that is honestly shareable: the same NAMES, the same PROPS
(adapted only where the platform forces it - `onPress` becomes `onClick`, `testID` becomes
`data-testid`), and the same COPY (§134's empty-state sentences, §135's error sentences). A
screen reader or a test author moving between the two codebases should recognise the shape
immediately.

Where this repo's Input differs from mobile's, it is because the platform forces it, not
because of an independent design decision:

- mobile's TextInput has no uncontrolled mode worth using, so Input there is `value`/
  `onChangeText`/`onBlur` driven by a `Controller`. A DOM `<input>` supports `ref` natively,
  so this Input is built with `forwardRef` and is meant to be spread from React Hook Form's
  `register()` directly (`<Input {...register('email')} />`), which is the idiomatic way to
  wire a web form and needs no `Controller` wrapper for a plain text/password field.
- `revealable` toggles `type="password"`/`type="text"` here instead of RN's
  `secureTextEntry`, and renders a `Show`/`Hide` toggle button rather than an icon tap target
  - same capability, platform-appropriate control.

Button's `pending`/`destructive` additions in this task are the same two decisions mobile's
Button already made (see that file's own docblock on why `destructive` is an enum arm with no
caller yet rather than an invented colour), carried over by name.
